====================================================================================
B. BEFORE BELIEVING A NULL: DOES THE TEST HAVE POWER?
====================================================================================
   Grouped CV holds out whole TEAMS. If sample composition barely varies
   inside a team, team-grouping removes exactly the variation the sample
   block needs, and the null above is about the design, not the world.

   mean share of a column's variance that is WITHIN team:
      SAMPLE-DEFINING columns  0.171   (55 columns)
      MODELLING columns        0.060   (28 columns)

   The worry was that low within-team variation makes the sample block
   unlearnable under team-grouped folds. It does not, and the internal
   control settles it: the MODELLING block has LESS within-team variation
   (0.060 vs 0.171) and still reaches R2 = 0.164 on log SE. Whatever stops the
   sample block from predicting the ESTIMATE, it is not team-grouping.
   (I wrote the opposite conclusion first, on a 0.25 threshold pulled from
    nowhere, and the control contradicted it. Left here as the correction.)

   Same table with folds drawn at MODEL level (ignoring team). This
   OVERSTATES everything -- models inside a team are near-duplicates -- and
   is shown only to bound how much signal exists at all:
   block                                           R2 estimate          R2 log SE
   SAMPLE-DEFINING (what counts as a case)              0.0135             0.4214
   MODELLING (fixed cases, different model)             0.0352             0.4035
   MEASUREMENT (which DV / which IV form)               0.0037             0.0780

